Issues.Body:
### Description

#6452 was fixed in `main` by #11521, but the fix is not in `release/10.0`. On the current .NET 10 runtime (Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App 10.0.13, WindowsBase 10.0.1326.50504), `Switch.MS.Internal.EnableWeakEventMemoryImprovements=true` still makes `WeakEventTable.Purge` throw `InvalidOperationException: Collection was modified`. The cleanup only has to run in a nested dispatcher frame while a weak event is being delivered through a manager that uses the base `WeakEventManager.Purge`. In that case `Purge` clones the in-use `ListenerList` and writes it back with `Table[this, source] = list`, which invalidates the in-place `Hashtable` enumerator.

Could #11521 be backported to .NET 10 servicing?

A realistic trigger: a `TextBox.Text` binding updates its source on `LostFocus` (delivered by `LostFocusEventManager`), and the view model setter shows a `MessageBox`. 15 built-in managers use the base `Purge`, including `CollectionChangedEventManager`, `CurrentChangedEventManager`, `LostFocusEventManager`, `CommandManager.RequerySuggestedEventManager` and `WeakEventManager<TSource, TEventArgs>`. The exception reaches `Dispatcher.UnhandledException`, so apps that opt into the switch crash.

### Reproduction Steps

`net10.0-windows` console app with `<UseWPF>true</UseWPF>`:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Threading;

internal static class Program
{
    private static readonly List<object> Keep = new();

    private sealed class Src
    {
        public event EventHandler<EventArgs> Evt;
        public void Raise() => Evt?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
    }

    private sealed class Listener
    {
        public void OnEvt(object s, EventArgs e) { }
    }

    [STAThread]
    private static void Main()
    {
        AppContext.SetSwitch("Switch.MS.Internal.EnableWeakEventMemoryImprovements", true);
        Dispatcher dispatcher = Dispatcher.CurrentDispatcher;
        dispatcher.UnhandledException += (_, e) => { Console.WriteLine(e.Exception); e.Handled = true; };

        var source = new Src();
        Keep.Add(source);
        EventHandler<EventArgs> handler = (_, _) =>
        {
            // A new listener schedules a cleanup; the nested frame (like MessageBox.Show) lets it run
            // while the ListenerList of `source` is still in use.
            WeakEventManager<Src, EventArgs>.AddHandler(new Src(), nameof(Src.Evt), new Listener().OnEvt);
            var frame = new DispatcherFrame();
            new DispatcherTimer(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1), DispatcherPriority.Normal, (_, _) => frame.Continue = false, dispatcher).Start();
            Dispatcher.PushFrame(frame);
        };
        Keep.Add(handler);
        WeakEventManager<Src, EventArgs>.AddHandler(source, nameof(Src.Evt), handler);

        dispatcher.BeginInvoke(() => { source.Raise(); dispatcher.BeginInvokeShutdown(DispatcherPriority.Normal); });
        Dispatcher.Run();
    }
}
```

If `Switch.MS.Internal.EnableCleanupSchedulingImprovements=true` is also set, the cleanup runs from `CleanupHelper.OnCleanupTick` after a GC. Add `GC.Collect(); GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();` before `PushFrame` to reproduce that path as well. We also reproduced it with `CollectionChangedEventManager` and with the `TextBox` + `LostFocus` + nested frame scenario above.

### Expected behavior

No exception. The in-use list is updated after the enumeration, as in `main` after #11521.

### Actual behavior

```
System.InvalidOperationException: Collection was modified; enumeration operation may not execute.
   at System.Collections.Hashtable.HashtableEnumerator.MoveNext()
   at MS.Internal.WeakEventTable.Purge(Boolean purgeAll)
   at MS.Internal.WeakEventTable.CleanupOperation(Object arg)
   at System.Windows.Threading.ExceptionWrapper.InternalRealCall(Delegate callback, Object args, Int32 numArgs)
   at System.Windows.Threading.ExceptionWrapper.TryCatchWhen(Object source, Delegate callback, Object args, Int32 numArgs, Delegate catchHandler)
```

With `EnableCleanupSchedulingImprovements` the frames are `CleanupHelper.DoCleanup` → `CleanupHelper.OnCleanupTick` → `DispatcherTimer.FireTick`.

### Regression?

No. The switch has behaved this way since it was introduced; #6452 reports the same on .NET 6 and .NET Framework 4.8.

### Known Workarounds

Leave `EnableWeakEventMemoryImprovements` off. `EnableCleanupSchedulingImprovements` alone is not affected: the legacy `Purge` copies the keys before enumerating.

### Impact

The scheduling switch alone already gives the large win in a WPF app with hundreds of thousands of weak listeners: 20 → 2–3 full table purges on 20 hover actions. We would also like to enable the memory switch, which roughly halved the cost of each purge in our synthetic test, but cannot until the fix ships in .NET 10.

### Configuration

- .NET 10.0.13 (Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App 10.0.13), x64
- Windows 10 Pro 22H2 (10.0.19045)
- `release/10.0` has no `_toUpdate` in `WeakEventTable.cs`; `main` has it since #11521

### Other information

The fix is #11521 (`WeakEventTable` defers `_dataTable` updates while `_inPurge` is set).
